  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nTonight through Saturday...\n\nLow clouds have struggled to dissipate completely this morning under \nthe blanket of a \"baroclinic leaf\" transitioning over the region \nthat is associated with a shortwave trough approaching the area. \nThis is also allowing for temperatures to be slightly cooler, and \nbelow the seasonal average for this time of year. There is some \nmoisture still lingering over the region, along with just enough \nforcing to maybe allow for an isolated shower/storm or two to pop \noff later this afternoon. However, there is not high confidence of \nthis since a majority of the instability is confined to the east of \nthe CWA. Nonetheless, an isolated shower/storm or two developing \nover the eastern San Bernardino Mountains and then moving out over \nthe high deserts cannot be ruled out. Some of the guidance does show \nthe potential of this, although the consensus, especially with the \nmid-level flow being out of the southwest, which is not so favorable \nas more dry air is becoming advected in gives more credence to \nconditions remaining dry for today and through tomorrow. Winds \nwill also increase later this afternoon across the higher terrain \nof possibly 50 mph or greater, and within the passes with possible\ngusts approaching 60 mph for Gorgonio Pass. This will also be the\ncase for tomorrow, except that winds will be slightly lighter \nthan this afternoon. Temperatures for tomorrow will also be \nslightly warmer, yet still below the seasonal average. The marine \nlayer should fill in better by later this afternoon and into the \nevening with the presence of better onshore flow. \n\nSunday through Thursday...\n\nThrough the weekend, there will be a gradual warmup as the ridge to \nthe east begins to expand back towards the west. Conditions should \nremain dry through then, as monsoonal moisture is suppressed \nfurther to the south. Temperatures will continue to warm into the \nearly half of next week as the ridge further builds, and there \ncould be pulses of monsoonal waves moving up from the southeast \naround the high centered over the desert southwest, which may \nincrease chances of getting afternoon thunderstorms over our \nmountains and deserts during the afternoon hours. Best chances at \nthis time look to be on Tuesday of next week, although even for \nthis day, the mid-level flow initially beginning out of the \nsoutheast earlier in the morning turns more southwesterly by later\nin the day, with less favorability for development. Temperatures \nwill return to around average through Wednesday, and then slowly \nbegin to drop off going into the latter half of next week as the \nridge weakens. Ensembles also reflect similar trends, with mostly \ndry conditions likely persisting through the end of next week.",
